#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Render the exact eighteen-qubit, four-dimensional second-order AC basis."""
from pathlib import Path
import json,re,html
HERE=Path(__file__).resolve().parent
ROOT=HERE.parents[1]
def tex(v,display=False):
    tag='div' if display else 'span'
    return '<'+tag+' class="math" data-tex="'+html.escape(v,quote=True)+'"'+(' data-display="true"' if display else '')+'></'+tag+'>'
def main():
    d=json.loads((HERE/'basis.json').read_text());r=json.loads((HERE/'verification.json').read_text());assert r['passed']
    css=re.search(r'<style>(.*?)</style>',(ROOT/'research/t2-spin2-inertia/index.html').read_text(),re.S).group(1)
    css='\n'.join(line.rstrip() for line in css.split('\n'))
    css+=' .basis-card{background:#fffef9;border:1px solid #deded3;border-radius:7px;padding:1.2rem;margin:1.5rem 0}.basis-card h3{margin-top:0}.downloads{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(220px,1fr));gap:1rem}.downloads a{display:block}.jump-links{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:1rem;margin:1.8rem 0}.math[data-display]{overflow-x:auto;overflow-y:hidden;padding:.5rem 0}section{scroll-margin-top:1rem}'
    body='<p class="eyebrow">Anticoherent subspaces · Exact radical basis</p><a class="back-link" href="../../index.html#n=18&amp;t=2&amp;subspace=radical-n18-k4-t2">← Return to the catalogue</a><h1>Eighteen qubits at second order</h1><p class="lead">Four analytical Dicke vectors span an optimal second-order AC subspace. Every normalized complex superposition has the required maximally mixed two-qubit reduction.</p><div class="result"><strong>Proved maximum dimension</strong>'+tex(r'\boxed{K(18,2)=4}',True)+'<p>The radical construction attains the auxiliary-spin upper bound.</p></div><nav class="jump-links" aria-label="On this page"><a href="#basis">Analytical basis</a><a href="#verification">Exact verification</a><a href="#optimality">Dimension proof</a><a href="#sources">Source and downloads</a></nav><section id="basis"><h2>An explicit orthonormal basis</h2><p>Write '+tex(r'D_r=|D_{18}^{(r)}\rangle')+' for normalized Dicke states. All other components vanish. Every square root is the positive real root.</p>'
    for i,b in enumerate(d['basis']):
        body+='<div class="basis-card"><h3>Basis vector '+str(i+1)+'</h3>'+tex(b['stateLatex'],True)
        if i<3:body+=tex(b['coefficientDefinitionLatex'],True)
        else:body+='<p>The same six coefficients '+tex(r'h_0,\ldots,h_5')+' appear in reverse Dicke order.</p>'
        body+='</div>'
    body+='<p>The first vector is reflection-even and the second reflection-odd; the last two are a reflected pair. These are convenient basis conventions, while maximality holds without any symmetry assumption.</p><p><a href="../../index.html#n=18&amp;t=2&amp;subspace=radical-n18-k4-t2">Inspect the coefficients and Majorana spheres ↗</a> · <a href="basis.json">Download all exact coefficients ↓</a></p></section><section id="verification"><h2>Why the entire four-dimensional span is AC</h2><p>The decisive identities include the ordered cross terms between different basis vectors:</p>'+tex(r'\langle u_a|u_b\rangle=\delta_{ab},\qquad R_2(|u_a\rangle\langle u_b|)=\delta_{ab}\frac{I_3}{3},\qquad1\le a,b\le4.',True)+'<p>They imply the condition for every normalized complex superposition:</p>'+tex(r'|\psi\rangle=\sum_{a=1}^4\alpha_a|u_a\rangle,\quad\sum_a|\alpha_a|^2=1\quad\Longrightarrow\quad\rho_2(\psi)=I_3/3.',True)+'<p>For a coefficient vector '+tex(r'c')+', normalized Dicke splitting gives</p>'+tex(r'(M_t(c))_{ab}=c_{a+b}\sqrt{\frac{\binom ta\binom{18-t}b}{\binom{18}{a+b}}},\qquad R_t(|u_a\rangle\langle u_b|)=M_t(u_a)M_t(u_b)^\dagger.',True)+'<p>Exact substitution checks all 144 complex entries of the sixteen ordered two-qubit cross reductions. The nonzero probabilities, in ascending Dicke order, are</p>'+tex(r'\begin{aligned}u_1:&\ (1810,885,2040,3652,2040,885,1810)/13122,\\u_2:&\ (6,5,16,16,5,6)/54,\\u_3:&\ (80,179,140,55,55,220)/729,\\u_4:&\ (220,55,55,140,179,80)/729.\end{aligned}',True)+'<p>The probability lists fix the diagonal moments. Support spacing three kills the first- and second-step self coherences. The radical phases also cancel every cross term, which is essential for a whole-subspace certificate.</p><h3>The displayed states have exactly order two</h3>'+tex(r'\operatorname{Tr}\rho_3(u_1)^2=\frac{39467}{147968},\quad\operatorname{Tr}\rho_3(u_2)^2=\frac{37267}{147968},',True)+tex(r'\operatorname{Tr}\rho_3(u_3)^2=\operatorname{Tr}\rho_3(u_4)^2=\frac{86707}{332928}.',True)+'<p>All four purities exceed '+tex(r'1/4')+'. This identifies the order of these basis vectors, without classifying every superposition.</p></section><section id="optimality"><h2>Why dimension four is optimal</h2><p>Let '+tex(r'P')+' project onto any second-order AC subspace of spin nine. The polarized moment identities give</p>'+tex(r'PJ_aP=0,\qquad P\frac{J_aJ_b+J_bJ_a}{2}P=30\delta_{ab}P.',True)+'<p>Couple to an auxiliary spin '+tex(r'3/2')+' and set '+tex(r'X=\mathbf J\cdot\mathbf S')+'. On the code times the four-dimensional auxiliary space, the compression of '+tex(r'X')+' is zero and that of '+tex(r'X^2')+' is '+tex(r'225I/2')+'. Therefore</p>'+tex(r'W=X^2-7X-\frac{225}{2}I',True)+'<p>has zero compression to a '+tex(r'4k')+'-dimensional subspace. Its spectrum, in increasing total-spin order, is</p>'+tex(r'\begin{array}{c|cccc}L&15/2&17/2&19/2&21/2\\\hline W_L&435/2&-99/4&-249/2&-99/4\\2L+1&16&18&20&22\end{array}',True)+'<p>There are sixteen positive and sixty negative eigenvalues, with no zero eigenvalues. Projection of a subspace with identically zero quadratic form into the positive eigenspace is injective: a nonzero vector with no positive component would have strictly negative quadratic form. Hence</p>'+tex(r'4k\le16\quad\Longrightarrow\quad k\le4.',True)+'<p>The analytical basis gives the matching lower bound. This is the specialization of the <a href="../ancilla-inertia/index.html">general auxiliary-spin theorem</a> and applies to arbitrary complex subspaces.</p></section><section id="sources"><h2>Source and reconstruction</h2><p>The starting point is John Martin’s '+tex(r'\Pi_{18,4,2}')+' numerical example in <a href="../../references/ACLiege.jl">ACLiege.jl, line 966</a>. Resolving the subspace into Dicke residues modulo three gives dimensions two, one and one. A common rotation aligns a half-turn with coefficient reversal; a unitary basis change selects the displayed even, odd and reflected vectors.</p><p>The simple rational probability lists reduce the remaining problem to ten phase variables. High-precision refinement reveals rational squares for all real and imaginary components. The displayed radical formulas are then certified by exact cross-reduction identities, independently of that numerical discovery.</p>'+tex(r'c_r\mapsto e^{-ir\beta/2}c_r,\qquad\beta\simeq0.734100176590464.',True)+'<p>After this common rotation, the numerical and analytical subspace projectors differ by less than '+tex(r'8.62\times10^{-8}')+' in Frobenius norm. This is approximate source association; the original and displayed bases are not claimed to be componentwise equal.</p><h3>Proof and reproducibility</h3><div class="downloads">'
    for label,path in [('Exact coefficients','basis.json'),('Complete LaTeX proof','proof.tex'),('Exact verifier','verify.py'),('Verification report','verification.json'),('Independent verifier','verify_independently.py'),('Independent report','independent-verification.json'),('Majorana coordinates','geometry.json'),('Geometry verifier','verify_geometry.py'),('Geometry report','geometry-verification.json'),('Page generator','build_page.py')]:body+='<a href="'+path+'">'+label+' ↓</a>'
    body+='</div><p>This proves an optimal analytical subspace, without classifying all optimal subspaces or claiming literature priority.</p></section><footer><a href="../../index.html#n=18&amp;t=2&amp;subspace=radical-n18-k4-t2">Return to the catalogue ↗</a></footer>'
    body=body.replace('<section id="sources">','<section><h2>Which first-order parents can contain this code?</h2><p>This four-dimensional second-order code has a largest containing first-order AC subspace of dimension exactly five, although K(18,1) = 7. <a href="../t2-nested-t1/index.html#example">Read the exact extension theorem and proof ↗</a></p></section><section id="sources">')
    head='<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>Eighteen qubits: an analytical four-dimensional second-order AC subspace</title><link rel="stylesheet" href="../../vendor/katex/katex.min.css"><script defer src="../../vendor/katex/katex.min.js"></script><style>'+css+'</style><script>document.addEventListener("DOMContentLoaded",()=>document.querySelectorAll("[data-tex]").forEach(el=>{if(window.katex)el.innerHTML=katex.renderToString(el.dataset.tex,{displayMode:el.dataset.display==="true",throwOnError:false,strict:"ignore",trust:false});}));</script></head><body><main>'
    (HERE/'index.html').write_text(head+body+'</main></body></html>\n')
if __name__=='__main__':main()
